  "description": "Compute scaled dot-product attention in pure NumPy using matrix multiplication and row-wise softmax.\n\nQueries act as lookup keys that match stored memory keys to retrieve weighted linear combinations of value vectors.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\operatorname{Attention}(Q, K, V) = \\operatorname{softmax}\\left( \\frac{Q K^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}} \\right) V\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Compute matrix product Q K^T scaled by 1/sqrt(d_k)\n2. Apply row-wise softmax normalization with numerical max subtraction\n3. Project attention probabilities across Value matrix V\n\n### Returns\nNested list representing attended output matrix.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Scaled Dot-Product Attention\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nLinear algebra is the foundational mathematical language of machine learning. Data samples, image pixels, and word embeddings are all represented as multidimensional vectors and matrices. **Scaled Dot-Product Attention** transforms coordinate systems, extracts principal axes of variation, or decomposes complex transformations into simpler geometric building blocks.\n\nQueries act as lookup keys that match stored memory keys to retrieve weighted linear combinations of value vectors. In this problem, the goal is to: compute scaled dot-product attention in pure numpy using matrix multiplication and row-wise softmax.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Attention}(Q, K, V) = \\operatorname{softmax}\\left( \\frac{Q K^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}} \\right) V\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Compute matrix product Q K^T scaled by 1/sqrt(d_k)\n2. **Phase 2**: Apply row-wise softmax normalization with numerical max subtraction\n3. **Phase 3**: Project attention probabilities across Value matrix V\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Feature Compression & Projections**: Operations like low-rank approximations and orthogonal projections compress high-dimensional feature spaces while preserving maximum variance.\n* **Numerical Stability**: When inverting matrices or solving linear systems, ill-conditioned matrices (matrices with very small determinants or high condition numbers) require regularization to prevent numerical instability.\n* **Vectorized Execution**: Expressing computations as matrix multiplications allows GPUs and CPUs to leverage hardware tensor cores for maximum throughput.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: Q = [[1.0, 0.0], [0.0, 1.0]], K = [[1.0, 0.0], [0.0, 1.0]], V = [[1.0, 2.0], [3.0, 4.0]]\n\nOutput: [[1.660477, 2.660477], [2.339523, 3.339523]]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated la_scaled_attention under standard test conditions."
